-- Q2867: Legal Duties and Responsibilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Ultramares doctrine (Ultramares Corp. v. Touche, 1931), consistent with Restatement (Second) of Torts Section 552, a CPA firm is liable for negligence only to parties in privity of contract or near-privity. The bank was not a party to the engagement and was unknown to the auditors, so it cannot recover for mere negligence.
Wrong (A): Reasonable reliance alone is insufficient under Ultramares. The bank must also be in privity or near-privity with the CPA firm.
Wrong (B): Negligence by the CPA firm does not automatically create liability to all parties. Under Ultramares, the duty of care extends only to those in a contractual relationship.
Wrong (D): While independent investigation is prudent, the bank''s failure to investigate is not the legal basis. The bar to recovery is the absence of privity under the Ultramares doctrine.$EXPL$
