-- Q2876: Legal Duties and Responsibilities [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the reasonably foreseeable user standard (Rosenblum v. Adler), recognized in Restatement (Second) of Torts Section 552, a CPA may be liable to any person whom the CPA could reasonably foresee would rely on the work product. Investors are reasonably foreseeable users of audited financial statements, making them eligible to sue.
Wrong (B): The privity requirement applies under the Ultramares doctrine, not the reasonably foreseeable user standard. This jurisdiction follows the broader approach.
Wrong (C): Actual fraud is not required under the foreseeable user standard. The CPA''s failure to detect a material fraud that a competent auditor would have found constitutes negligence.
Wrong (D): The CPA did not commit the fraud but failed to detect it. A CPA''s duty under auditing standards includes designing procedures to detect material fraud.$EXPL$
